# glm-prestage-one-array.patch (sync, 2026-10-29; no production counterpart): a waiting request's prompt ids are made
# once (multi.stream_ids: a list of ints and an int64 array, kept on the stream for the prompt object it holds) and
# read by prefix_wait, prestage and admit; BlockStore.best takes the caller's array. On the synced base, upstream's
# spill prestage (recipe 0128) converted the prompt with a list comprehension and then to an array twice (_resume and
# best) before admit converted it again (OTS 0124's one array a request, undone by the code in front of it).
# Measured on the real functions (prestage with nothing on disk, then admit's ids; workstation CPU, 600,000 ids): 66 ms
# -> 26 ms of rank 0's host before the ADMIT, with the spill tier on (prestage returns at once without it). The same
# ids and arrays: every decision is unchanged.
# Test: tests/host/test_prestage_ids.py (CPU; one conversion at 600k through prestage and admit's read, a new prompt
# object converted again, best's answer the same with and without the caller's array); mutants:
# tests/host/mutants_prestage_ids.py (5 of 5 killed). Applies after glm-fill-fast-ported.patch.
